# TODO - Malbolge machine-model and C compilation paper

## Objective

Publish the systems/compiler result as a reproducible study of compiling a
modern deterministic C profile to Malbolge's versioned execution model, using
an explicit abstract-machine description rather than relying on the historical
"esoteric programming language" label as the technical model.

## Acceptance

- Historical sources and repository specifications distinguish the traditional
  language label from the formal object used by the compiler and runtime.
- The paper models the target as a ternary, writable-code abstract machine with
  position-dependent decode, the normative crazy operation, post-instruction
  encryption/self-modification, and versioned execution geometry.
- The paper does not claim that Malbolge is literally "not a language"; it
  explains why an ISA/abstract-machine framing is more useful for compilation,
  verification, and reproducible execution while preserving historical usage.
- The systems claim centers on deterministic C-to-Malbolge compilation,
  execution, verification, substantial-program evidence, and the limits of the
  supported profile rather than on novelty claims about cryptographic hardware.
- Comparisons to encrypted-memory processors, ASLR, enclaves, or
  cryptographically protected execution are used only when a cited source
  supports the specific analogy; they are not asserted as semantic equivalence.
- The declared evidence is reproducible and separates measured capability from
  interpretation, historical framing, and broader Church-Turing commentary.
- Completion of the separate effective-transcendental-separation paper is not a
  prerequisite for this paper or for ordinary product engineering.

## Evidence

- Primary target semantics come from the versioned Malbolge specifications,
  execution traces, differential VM evidence, and compiler artifacts already
  governed by the repository.
- Required systems evidence includes the turnkey C workflow, substantial
  generated programs, provenance-preserving historical comparison, resource
  accounting, and explicit threats to validity.
- The paper pipeline owns publication packaging; this TODO owns the thesis and
  evidence boundary for the systems/compiler paper.
